# Cursor Rules

## Code Conciseness

- Write concise, readable code. Prefer clarity over verbosity.
- Avoid unnecessary comments. Code should be self-documenting.
- Use descriptive variable names, but keep them short when context is clear.
- Prefer early returns and guard clauses over nested conditionals.
- Use modern JavaScript features (arrow functions, destructuring, optional chaining, nullish coalescing).
- Extract repeated logic into reusable functions/components, but avoid premature abstraction.

## Code Style

- Use ES6+ modules (import/export) consistently.
- Use const by default, let only when reassignment is needed, avoid var.
- Use template literals for string interpolation.
- Prefer async/await over Promise chains.
- Use optional chaining (?.) and nullish coalescing (??) for safe property access.
- Prefer functional programming patterns (map, filter, reduce) over imperative loops when appropriate.

## React/Next.js Best Practices

- Use functional components with hooks. Avoid class components.
- Keep components small and focused on a single responsibility.
- Extract complex logic into custom hooks.
- Use "use client" directive only when necessary (client-side interactivity, hooks, browser APIs, Context API).
- Keep server components as the default. Move to client components only when needed.
- Use Next.js built-in features (Image, Link, Script) over generic HTML elements.
- Handle loading and error states explicitly.
- Use TanStack Query (React Query) for data fetching and caching.
- Use Context API for shared state (like authentication).
- Use default exports for React components.
- Use clsx for conditional CSS classes.
- Use React Bootstrap components and Bootstrap utility classes for styling.

## Node.js/Express Best Practices

- Use async/await for asynchronous operations. Handle errors with try/catch.
- Always validate user input using express-validator.
- Use environment variables for configuration. Never hardcode secrets.
- Implement proper error handling with appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Use middleware for cross-cutting concerns (auth, logging, validation).
- Keep route handlers focused. Extract business logic into service modules.
- Use structured logging (via logger service) instead of console.log in production code.
- Use express-session for session management. Configure DynamoDB store for production.
- Use passport.js with OIDC strategies for authentication.
- Use multer for file uploads with custom storage implementations.
- Use compression middleware for response compression.
- Use CORS middleware appropriately.

## Error Handling

- Handle errors explicitly. Don't silently swallow exceptions.
- Provide meaningful error messages for debugging, but sanitize for production.
- Use appropriate HTTP status codes.
- Log errors with sufficient context using the logger service.
- Use error boundaries in React for graceful error handling.
- Use express-validator's handleValidationErrors middleware for validation errors.

## Security

- Never expose secrets, API keys, or credentials in code or version control.
- Validate and sanitize all user inputs using express-validator.
- Implement proper authentication and authorization checks using passport.js.
- Use HTTPS in production.
- Set secure cookie flags (httpOnly, secure, sameSite) in session configuration.
- Store OAuth state and nonce in session, not in URL parameters.
- Keep dependencies up to date and audit for vulnerabilities.

## Performance

- Avoid unnecessary re-renders in React. Use React.memo, useMemo, useCallback judiciously.
- Use TanStack Query's caching and refetching strategies appropriately.
- Use compression middleware for API responses.
- Avoid blocking the event loop with synchronous operations.
- Use Next.js middleware for route protection instead of client-side checks.

## Code Organization

- Group related functionality together.
- Keep files focused. Split large files into smaller modules.
- Use consistent naming conventions (camelCase for variables/functions, PascalCase for components/classes).
- Place imports in this order: external packages, internal modules, relative imports.
- Use default exports for React components and main module exports.
- Use named exports for utilities, services, and shared functions.
- Organize server code into services (auth, db, api, etc.).
- Organize client code into components, contexts, and services.

## Session Management

- Use express-session with DynamoDB store in production, memory store in development.
- Store OAuth state, nonce, and code verifier in session during authentication flow.
- Clean up session data after authentication completes.
- Use session.touch() to extend session expiration on activity.
- Configure session cookies with appropriate security flags based on environment.

## File Uploads

- Use multer with custom storage implementations for file uploads.
- Handle chunked uploads when dealing with large files.
- Validate file types and sizes before processing.
- Clean up temporary files after processing.

## Environment Configuration

- Use environment variables for all configuration (see example.env).
- Never commit .env files or secrets to version control.
- Use example.env files to document required environment variables.
- Validate required environment variables at application startup.

## API Design

- Use RESTful conventions for API endpoints.
- Return consistent JSON response formats.
- Use appropriate HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE).
- Use express-validator for request validation.
- Implement proper error responses with appropriate status codes.

## General Principles

- Write code for humans first, computers second.
- Prefer explicit over implicit.
- Don't repeat yourself (DRY), but don't abstract prematurely.
- Keep it simple. Avoid over-engineering.
- Refactor when code smells appear, but don't refactor working code unnecessarily.
- Follow the principle of least surprise.
- Make it work, make it right, make it fast (in that order).
